<section xml:id="HW9" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Homework 9</title>

  <p><alert> Due Thursday, April 9</alert></p>

<exercises xml:id="hw9-ex">
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
        [From the book]: Chapter 15: 2.1, 2.2, 3.1
        </p>
    </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
We say that <m> \alpha</m> generates a field extension <m>F\subseteq K</m> if <m>K=F(\alpha)</m> . Thus, for problem 3.1 you need to show <m>F(\alpha)=F(\alpha^2)</m>. (Hint: Use the multiplicative property of degree.)
        </p>
    </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
        <p>
          [Problem A] The Structure theorem of finite abelian groups says that any 
           finite abelian group can be written (up to isomorphism) as a direct product of cyclic groups of the form 
           <m>\mathbb Z_{p^n}</m> with <m>p</m> prime. If <m>G</m> is a finite abelian group,
            <m>p</m> is a prime number and <m>n</m> is a non-negative integer, we let 
           <m>r_G(p,n)</m> denote the number of times that
             <m>\mathbb Z_{p^n}</m> appears in this decomposition of <m>G</m> as a direct product. The uniqueness part of the theorem tells us 
             that two groups <m>G</m> and  <m>G'</m> are isomorphic if and only if 
             <m>r_G(p,n)=r_{G'}(p,n)</m> for all primes <m>p</m> and all non-negative integers <m>n</m>. 
             (This is equivalent to saying that such decompositions are unique, up to the order in which we write the terms of 
             the direct products.)
        </p>
        <p>
             Use the theorem and the notation introduced above to show the following hold for <m>M,N,P</m> finite abelian groups:
        </p>
    <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
              If <m>M\times M\cong N\times N</m> then <m>M\cong N</m>.  
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                If <m> M\times N\cong M\times P</m> then <m>N\cong P</m>.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
    <p>
[Problem B] If <m>F\subseteq K</m> is a  field extension and <m>\alpha\in K</m>, then <m>F(\alpha)</m> is the set of all fractions in <m>K</m> of the form <m>\frac{f(\alpha)}{g(\alpha)}</m>, where <m>f,g\in F[x]</m>.  
    </p>
    <p>
If <m>F\subseteq L</m> is another field extension, show: If <m>\alpha\in L</m>, then <m>F(\alpha)\subseteq L</m>. 
    </p>
    <p>
Then, use this observation to prove
<me>\mathbb Q(\sqrt{2})=\mathbb Q(2+3\sqrt{2})</me>.
    </p>
</exercise>

</exercises>
</section>